WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.940
Top management cannot delegate an AIMS entirely to an AI governance specialist.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:19.800
A specialist can design processes and coordinate evidence, but leadership decides priorities, resources, risk appetite, escalation, and how AI governance integrates with business operations.

3
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:26.220
When executives ask only for a certification binder, teams learn that paperwork matters more than controlled decisions.

4
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:34.800
Leadership is visible when managers resolve conflicts between speed, assurance, customer commitments, and affected-party protections.

5
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.040
The AI policy should express organizational direction for AI within the AIMS scope.

6
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:49.420
A useful policy is appropriate to the organization's purpose, AI activities, risks, impacts, commitments, and improvement expectations.

7
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:56.160
Words such as transparency, fairness, accountability, and human oversight are not enough by themselves.

8
00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:04.580
The policy has to point toward procedures, decision rights, communication, monitoring, and evidence that people can actually operate.

9
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:10.120
If a policy commitment cannot be traced to ownership or practice, it is probably still aspirational.

10
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.640
Roles turn policy into action.

11
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:33.220
A mature AIMS may identify a top-management sponsor, AIMS owner, AI-system owner, product owner, model owner, data owner, risk owner, security and privacy personnel, legal and compliance stakeholders, procurement, internal audit, human-oversight personnel, and incident owners.

12
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.400
Responsibility means a person performs or manages work.

13
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.940
Accountability means an authorized person answers for the result and ensures decisions are made.

14
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:50.400
One person can hold multiple roles in a smaller organization, but authority and escalation still need to be explicit.

15
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:56.120
AIMS objectives translate direction into measurable or evaluable commitments.

16
00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:11.740
Examples might include completing impact assessments for defined systems, improving supplier due diligence, increasing monitoring coverage, closing corrective actions within target timeframes, reducing unapproved AI use, or improving role-based competence.

17
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:19.440
Each objective needs an owner, timeframe, resources, method of evaluation, and a way to update it when context changes.

18
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:24.040
Support requirements keep governance from becoming symbolic.

19
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:31.660
Resources include people, tools, budget, data access, review time, and management attention.

20
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.240
Competence is demonstrated ability, not only attendance at training.

21
00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:41.860
Awareness means people understand how their work affects responsible AI outcomes.

22
00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:49.960
Communication should define what is shared, with whom, when, by whom, through which channel, and with what escalation.

23
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:56.220
Documented information provides consistency and evidence, but it should be controlled so teams use current versions.

24
00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:05.280
Governance becomes operational when policy, authority, competence, communication, and resources align.

25
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:15.540
In practice, this alignment is what lets a product team, procurement lead, data owner, and risk owner make consistent decisions under pressure instead of improvising each time.

26
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.520
That consistency is the difference between named governance and actual governance.
